// Test simfile with hand-worked beats after stops, for calibrating ddr-trainer's chart
// analyzer (src/chart_analyzer.rs), whose tests check the tables below. Generated by
// test_resources/make_test_simfiles.py, so edit that instead of this.
//
// 120 BPM, and four stops, which all the charts share:
//   beat     4        12        13       20
//   seconds  0.25     0.375     0.25     0.3
//   beats    1/2      3/4       1/2      0.6, not a length of music
//
// A stop that isn't a whole number of beats long either leaves the beat where it's written
// (the music waits too) or moves it by its own length (the music goes on), and nothing in
// the timing says which. The notes do: each stretch of notes between two stops takes, of the
// shifts the stops before it could add up to (any of them, each rounded to a 48th of a beat),
// the one that puts its notes on the coarsest grids, adding up 1 for a note on the beat, 2
// for an 8th, 4 for a 16th. On a tie it stays with the stretch before, or else takes all the
// stops since. A note on a stop's beat is before the stop. Where a note sits against the
// beat (its subdivision, off the beat or not, a syncopation or not) is read from where it is
// heard: its beat plus that shift.
//
//   chart      notes  off-beat      syncopations   finest subdivision
//              heard  (as written)  heard (written)  heard (written)
//   Beginner    0     0             0                0                 nothing in it
//   Easy       10     1/10 (4/10)   1 (3)            8 (8)             the beat moves
//   Medium      9     0 (0)         0 (0)            4 (4)             a pause
//   Hard        9     0 (4/9)       0 (4)            4 (16)            two stops add up
//   Challenge  15     0 (10/15)     0 (10)           4 (8)             one stop of two
//
// Easy: 0 1 2 3 4, then 4.5 5.5 6.5 7.5 8 after the stop on 4. As written those five add up
//   to 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 9, and half a beat on (5 6 7 8 8.5) to 6, so the stop moved the
//   beat. The 8.5 is off the beat with nothing after it: a syncopation, 0.25 s after the note
//   before, so at 4 a second. The note on 4 is before the stop.
// Medium: 0 1 2 3 4, then 5 6 7 8, on the beat as written (4, against 8 half a beat on). The
//   same stop, but here the music waited.
// Hard: 8 9 10 11 12, then 13.75 14.75 15.75 16.75 after the stops on 12 and 13, with no note
//   between the two. The stops so far can add up to 0, 1/4, 1/2 or 3/4 of a beat more than
//   whole beats. A quarter on puts them on the beat (4, against 16 as written), which is what
//   both stops since come to (3/4 + 1/2), so they are heard 1 1/4 beats on: 15 16 17 18. Its
//   first five notes are after the stop on 4, and on the beat as written.
// Challenge: 8 9 10 11 12, then 13.5 to 19.5 every beat, then 20.5 21.5 22.5 after the stop
//   on 20. Half a beat on puts 13.5 to 19.5 on the beat (7, against 14 as written, and 28 a
//   quarter on, where both stops would put them), so one of the two stops moved the beat:
//   14 to 20. After the stop on 20 the same half beat still fits (3, against 6 as written and
//   144 at 0.6 of a beat more), so the beat stays: 21 22 23.
#TITLE:Beat Stats;
#ARTIST:ddr-trainer;
#MUSIC:beat_stats.ogg;
#OFFSET:0.000000;
#BPMS:0.000=120.000;
#STOPS:4.000=0.250,12.000=0.375,13.000=0.250,20.000=0.300;

//---------------dance-single - nothing in it----------------
#NOTES:
     dance-single:
     nothing in it:
     Beginner:
     1:
     0.412,0.356,0.250,0.100,0.000:
// measure 0: empty
0000
0000
0000
0000
,  // measure 1: empty
0000
0000
0000
0000
;

//---------------dance-single - the beat moves----------------
#NOTES:
     dance-single:
     the beat moves:
     Easy:
     3:
     0.412,0.356,0.250,0.100,0.000:
// measure 0: quarter notes
1000
0100
0010
0001
,  // measure 1: one on the stop's beat, then four half a beat late as written
1000
0100
0000
0010
0000
0001
0000
1000
,  // measure 2: on the beat as written: heard half a beat after it
1000
0000
0000
0000
;

